  "worked_solution": "The Cartesian product $A\\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B={(a,b)\\,:\\,a\\in A,\\,b\\in B}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set \n\n$$\nS={Z}^{-}\\times {Z}^{-}\n$$\n\ncontains every point in ${R}^{2}$ whose $x$-coordinate is a negative integer and whose $y$-coordinate is a negative integer.\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\in {Z}^{-},\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider ${S}_{-1}\u2282S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is negative integer.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-1}={(-1,y)\\,:\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-1}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/37ad5627c3fb7cc89e751bb697e01ac7fecaf2712e230b6e3812ad9137d5cd46.png)\n\nNext, consider ${S}_{-2}\u2282S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals $-2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any negative integer.\n\n$$\n{S}_{-2}={(-2,y)\\,:\\,y\\in {Z}^{-}}.\n$$\n\nWe can represent ${S}_{-2}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/97ebcac85477b8e88564ab67d59c926d5faa72138c8a31d80d3b89895f33668c.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $\u2026{S}_{-5},{S}_{-4},{S}_{-3}$ similarly. Our Cartesian product $S$ is the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =\u2026{S}_{-5}\\cup {S}_{-4}\\cup {S}_{-3}\\cup {S}_{-2}\\cup {S}_{-1} \\\\  & ={\u22c3}_{i\\in {Z}^{-}}{S}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize ${Z}^{-}\\times {Z}^{-}$ as the set of points in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082768/assets/a756e53554cb9184d96a52b83038fbd6b00b21d0a12cc658aebb5010e917fd03.png)",
